nodecode-image-utils is a rewrite of MazenMkhinini's MazenMkhinini/hermes-image-plugin:

  https://github.com/MazenMkhinini/hermes-image-plugin
  commit c4123fbdb315137b40e4c5b4b5fd3eb2298dae66
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 MazenMkhinini (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the six tools keep their names, their arguments, their
refusals and their defaults - the overwrite-and-confirm gate, the
`<stem>_<operation>.<ext>` name written beside the input, one of
width/height/percent with the shape kept, a crop box checked against the
bounds, EXIF orientation handled before an angle, alpha into JPEG needing
`flatten`, and an optimize that reports the bytes it moved - but every line
of them is Common Lisp here rather than Python: the decoding and encoding
sit on the engine's own image codecs (PNG, GIF, WebP and JPEG, the ones a
vision attachment goes through), and BMP is read and written in the cell.
Pillow is gone, and with it the resampling vocabulary it named: `lanczos`
averages every source pixel a result covers, `bicubic` and `bilinear`
interpolate, `nearest` takes one sample.

What was left out, and why: the original's animated formats (GIF, WebP,
TIFF, AVIF) are read but not written, because every frame of an animation
is a palette and a timing this cell would have to encode itself, and it
writes the three formats a person asks for - PNG, JPEG and BMP - naming the
others as not written instead of guessing. Interlaced and 16-bit PNG and
non-baseline JPEG are read through the engine's readers rather than
re-implemented. The bundled skill and the install.sh helper have no
counterpart: a Nodecode cell needs neither, since its tools are registered
with the harness and its folder is the install.
